---
import CiteBox from "../components/CiteBox.astro";
import {
	APPROACH_COMPARISON,
	APPROACH_HEADING,
	APPROACH_LEAD,
	APPROACH_PIPELINE,
} from "../data/approach";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { COPY } from "../seo/copy";
---

<Layout title={COPY.approach.title} description={COPY.approach.description}>
	<article class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-12 sm:px-6">
		<header class="max-w-3xl">
			<p class="eyebrow">Approach</p>
			<h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-4xl font-medium leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">{APPROACH_HEADING}</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{APPROACH_LEAD}</p>
			<p class="mt-5 text-sm text-ink-3">John J. Hughes, III</p>
		</header>

		<div class="mt-12 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-4" aria-label="From public filings to evaluation signal">
			{APPROACH_PIPELINE.map((item, i) => (
				<div class="card relative p-5">
					<p class="font-serif text-sm text-accent">0{i + 1}</p>
					<h2 class="mt-2 font-serif text-lg font-semibold">{item.step}</h2>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{item.body}</p>
					{i < APPROACH_PIPELINE.length - 1 && <span class="absolute -right-2.5 top-1/2 hidden -translate-y-1/2 text-rule-strong md:block" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
				</div>
			))}
		</div>

		<div class="prose-lfb prose mt-14 max-w-[46rem] prose-headings:scroll-mt-24">
			<h2 id="private-data">The private-data problem</h2>
			<p>
				Most legal work product never becomes public. Memos, deal documents, and client advice are privileged or confidential, so there is no large, open corpus of expert legal work with known-good answers. Evaluations have filled the gap with rubrics: experts write criteria for what a good answer contains, and model judges grade responses against them. That approach is useful, but the answer key is only as good as the people who wrote it, and it cannot tell you whether the advice would have held up in the real world.
			</p>

			<h2 id="public-litigation">What public litigation exposes</h2>
			<p>
				Federal litigation is different. When a defendant moves to dismiss, the public docket can contain the complaint, motion, opposition, and reply, followed by a judge's written decision. Cases with a usable record and a written ruling give us three things that are rare elsewhere in law:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>The inputs.</strong> We can reconstruct the filed arguments and record the decision-maker had in front of them, checking for missing or sealed material.</li>
				<li><strong>An objective outcome.</strong> Whether each challenged claim against each defendant was dismissed is a matter of record, not opinion.</li>
				<li><strong>The reasoning.</strong> The judge's opinion states which arguments mattered and why, in the judge's own words.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Together, these turn a high-level legal judgment task into one that can be checked. Predicting how a judge will rule is the kind of call senior litigators are paid to make, and here the answer eventually arrives. Because new decisions keep coming, each benchmark version can use rulings issued after a model's documented training cutoff. That is our eligibility rule, not proof that a model has never encountered an answer during training or post-training. Limited contamination remains a working assumption; unknown or overlapping cutoffs require visible qualification.
			</p>

			<h2 id="curation">Raw data is not enough</h2>
			<p>
				Public does not mean ready. Building this benchmark showed how much careful work it takes to turn court records into a reliable answer key:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Some court documents must be purchased when free copies are unavailable, and many cases turn out to be unsuitable once examined.</li>
				<li>Docket entries are frequently mislabeled, so confirming that the test record contains everything the judge saw takes several quality-control steps.</li>
				<li>Even frontier models often disagree about how to read a ruling, or about which claims and defendants a motion actually challenged. Specifying the task and translating the judge's decision into an answer key requires legal judgment.</li>
				<li>Definitions matter. A dismissal with leave to amend counts as dismissal; a motion mooted because the plaintiff amended first does not. Models that blur that line lose points for the wrong reason.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The signal is real, but it has to be derived. The research contribution is less the raw data than the curation that makes it trustworthy.
			</p>

			<h2 id="training">From evaluation to training</h2>
			<p>
				If outcomes can be checked, prediction might also serve as a reinforcement-learning environment with verifiable rewards. My hypothesis is that teaching models to forecast real rulings could improve something current models lack: a sense of which issues actually decide a case. Today's models are excellent at research and summarizing doctrine, but when asked for strategic advice they tend to dwell on minor, technical points. Judges' written reasoning offers a second signal beyond the bare outcome: which arguments moved the court.
			</p>
			<p>
				This is a hypothesis, not a result. The public benchmark uses an AI labeling pipeline with quality controls; a separate, hand-labeled training set is being built to test whether properly labeled litigation data provides a training signal that generalizes beyond prediction. The evaluation corpus is not used to train or fine-tune models.
			</p>

			<h2 id="outcomes-vs-rubrics">Outcome grading and rubric grading</h2>
			<p>
				The two approaches measure different things and are complementary. Rubrics can assess the quality of a deliverable, which an outcome cannot. Outcomes can assess whether judgment tracks reality, which a rubric cannot. They also fail in different ways.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="card mt-6 max-w-4xl overflow-hidden">
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-[13px] outline-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-[-3px] focus-visible:outline-accent" role="region" aria-label="Comparison of outcome and rubric grading" tabindex="0">
				<table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
					<caption class="sr-only">Comparison of outcome-based and rubric-based evaluation</caption>
					<thead class="border-b border-rule text-xs text-ink-3">
						<tr>
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium"><span class="sr-only">Dimension</span></th>
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Outcome grading</th>
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Rubric grading</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-rule">
						{APPROACH_COMPARISON.map(([dimension, outcome, rubric]) => (
							<tr>
								<th scope="row" class="w-44 px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-ink">{dimension}</th>
								<td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-ink-2">{outcome}</td>
								<td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-ink-2">{rubric}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="prose-lfb prose mt-10 max-w-[46rem] prose-headings:scroll-mt-24">
			<h2 id="rubric-failures">How rubric answer keys fail</h2>
			<p>
				The problems below are not specific to any one benchmark; they can arise wherever a rubric serves as the answer key. To make them concrete, I read two tasks from Harvey's Legal Agent Benchmark (LAB) closely, checking each grading criterion against the task's record and the governing law. LAB is a set of long-horizon legal work-product tasks graded against expert-written criteria, and its tasks and scoring code are <a href="https://github.com/harveyai/harvey-labs">public</a>, so anyone can check what I describe. Most issue-spotting criteria were sound. But a meaningful share had one of five problems. The examples come from those two LAB tasks; I describe them without reproducing the task text. A wider, AI-assisted audit of all 52 LAB litigation tasks, with my own review of 25 sampled criteria, is browsable in the <a href="/lab/">LAB explorer</a>.
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<strong>The key can pull in conflicting directions.</strong> One criterion favored Georgia law for the tort claims; another required an argument that a merger clause could not bar fraudulent inducement under Georgia or Texas law. Under Georgia law, a party that affirms a contract containing a merger clause is generally barred from claiming reliance on outside representations (<em>Ekeledo v. Amporful</em>, 281 Ga. 817 (2007); <em>Novare Grp., Inc. v. Sarif</em>, 290 Ga. 186 (2011)). Rescission, the clause's wording, and the choice of law matter. A supported Texas-law argument can satisfy the second criterion, but a memo should also receive credit for explaining why the categorical Georgia-law argument fails.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The key rewards the weaker argument.</strong> A criterion required attacking a one-year contractual limitations period as unconscionable. A federal court applying Georgia law rejected an unconscionability challenge to a commercial contract's 60/90-day reporting-and-claim deadlines (<a href="https://openjurist.org/315/fsupp3d/1342/cobra-tactical-inc-v-payment-alliance-int-l-inc"><em>Cobra Tactical, Inc. v. Payment Alliance Int'l, Inc.</em>, 315 F. Supp. 3d 1342 (N.D. Ga. 2018)</a>). Texas generally makes contractual suit periods shorter than two years void (<a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/CP/htm/CP.16.htm#16.070">Tex. Civ. Prac. &amp; Rem. Code § 16.070</a>), subject to its business-sale exception and applicable special statutes. Whether that rule applies requires classifying the transaction and resolving the governing law. The statutory argument deserved consideration, but the criterion rewarded only unconscionability.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The key requires facts that are not in the record.</strong> One criterion required a specific court deadline that appears nowhere in the task's documents. It can be satisfied only by inventing the date.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The key grades what was never requested.</strong> Several criteria required severity ratings, spreadsheet layouts, or risk labels that the prompt never asked for. A memo that answered the question as posed fails them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Strict scoring amplifies every error.</strong> When a response scores only if every criterion passes, a single defective criterion turns a correct answer into a zero. LAB uses this <a href="https://github.com/harveyai/harvey-labs/blob/main/docs/eval-strategies.md">all-pass scoring</a>: a task scores 1 only if every criterion passes, and in the standard setup two model judges grade each response independently and their task scores are averaged.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				There is also a subtler issue. Rubrics built from long checklists reward recall: flag every issue the authors listed. Practicing lawyers often value the opposite, a short list of dispositive points without the technically accurate but minor ones. A model can learn to satisfy the checklist without getting better at that judgment.
			</p>
			<p>
				None of this makes rubric benchmarks worthless. They measure things outcomes cannot, and their authors are doing hard work. But an answer key deserves the review we would give a brief. Grading against what courts actually decided avoids prescribing the argument a model must make, though our own outcome labels still need review.
			</p>

			<h2 id="limits">What outcome grading does not solve</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Prediction is not the same as reasoning.</strong> A model could forecast well by modeling a particular judge's tendencies rather than the law. I expect legal analysis to dominate for ordinary motions, but that is a hypothesis this benchmark does not yet test.</li>
				<li><strong>Outcomes are noisy.</strong> Reasonable judges disagree, and one ruling is a single draw. That is why the benchmark scores probabilities rather than right-or-wrong calls.</li>
				<li><strong>The task is narrow.</strong> Motions to dismiss are one decision point in one court system. Other verifiable slices of law will need their own careful definitions.</li>
				<li><strong>Versions are not directly comparable.</strong> Each version uses new cases, which keeps it fresh but means scores measure a point in time.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				For how the benchmark handles these limits in practice, see the <a href="/methods/">methods</a>. For the current results, see the <a href="/">leaderboard</a>.
			</p>
		</div>

		<CiteBox class="mt-16 max-w-3xl" />
	</article>
</Layout>
